# Build context for the walker2d-viz demo image (landing/walker2d-viz/server/Dockerfile).
#
# The context is the REPO ROOT, because the image has to build the `spiky_cuda` extension from
# native/spiky and import the `spiky` package from src/ -- both outside the old ./server
# context. This file is therefore an ALLOW-LIST: deny everything, then re-admit only what the
# build actually needs. Without it the daemon would receive the whole repo, including
# experiments/walker2d_lut (1.1 GB) and experiments/walker2d-lut (484 MB).
*

# --- the demo server itself
!landing/walker2d-viz/server/**

# --- the native extension sources + the codegen the setup.py runs
!native/spiky/**
!native/common/**

# --- the python package the actor imports (spiky.spnet, spiky.util)
!src/spiky/**

# --- root packaging metadata that `pip install ./native/spiky` and the package need
!setup.py
!requirements.txt

# --- and now carve the heavy, irrelevant parts back OUT of those allowed trees.
# Test fixtures dominate src/: 30 MB of reference voltages + 64 MB of MNIST, none of it used
# at runtime.
src/spiky/**/tests/**

# STALE LOCAL BUILD ARTEFACTS -- THIS EXCLUSION IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT TIDINESS.
# A developer machine with a GPU leaves a CUDA-linked spiky_cuda*.so and a populated build/
# tree here. If either reached the image, the container could end up with the CUDA variant of
# the extension -- which imports fine on the build host and fails inside the GPU-less
# container. The image must compile its own CPU extension from source, every time.
native/spiky/build/**
native/**/*.so
native/**/*.o
**/__pycache__/**
**/*.pyc
**/*.egg-info/**
